WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.601 --> 00:00:04.003
-Our next guest is an Emmy- and
Golden Globe-nominated actor

00:00:04.003 --> 00:00:05.972
you know, from shows like
"Escape at Dannemora,"

00:00:05.972 --> 00:00:07.807
as well as the films
"Little Miss Sunshine,"

00:00:07.807 --> 00:00:09.609
"There Will Be Blood"
and "The Batman."

00:00:09.609 --> 00:00:12.345
This morning, he was nominated
for a Critics' Choice Award

00:00:12.345 --> 00:00:13.846
for his work in "The Fabelmans,"

00:00:13.846 --> 00:00:15.348
which is in theaters
and on demand now.

00:00:15.348 --> 00:00:16.382
Let's take a look.

00:00:16.382 --> 00:00:18.851
-$100 for a hobby?

00:00:18.851 --> 00:00:20.353
-It's not a hobby, Dad.

00:00:20.353 --> 00:00:22.321
-If you spent half the time
on algebra that you spend on --

00:00:22.321 --> 00:00:23.990
-Algebra? I hate algebra.

00:00:23.990 --> 00:00:25.858
Why are you...

00:00:25.858 --> 00:00:27.560
[ Chuckles ]
It's completely pointless.

00:00:27.560 --> 00:00:29.996
-Not if you want to make
something, it's not pointless.

00:00:29.996 --> 00:00:31.831
Geez. Sammy, when I was a boy,

00:00:31.831 --> 00:00:34.701
I always used to think somebody
figured out how to make this.

00:00:34.701 --> 00:00:38.537
This car, that rear-view mirror,
that directional signal.

00:00:38.537 --> 00:00:40.540
-I want to make movies, though.

00:00:40.540 --> 00:00:43.943
-Please welcome back the show
our friend Paul Dano, everybody!

00:00:43.943 --> 00:00:46.846
[ Cheers and applause ]
♪♪

00:01:03.429 --> 00:01:04.664
Welcome back!

00:01:04.664 --> 00:01:06.966
-Janelle danced out here.
-I know.

00:01:06.966 --> 00:01:08.634
-She's got the way
to walk onstage.

00:01:08.634 --> 00:01:10.403
-We should have
let you go first.

00:01:10.403 --> 00:01:11.904
-I know.

00:01:11.904 --> 00:01:14.907
-When you saw her outfit, you
were probably like, "Oh, geez."

00:01:14.907 --> 00:01:16.241
-I tried to change.

00:01:16.241 --> 00:01:19.745
I thought if I just take
the shirt off and go, you know,

00:01:19.745 --> 00:01:21.080
just cardigan.

00:01:21.080 --> 00:01:22.182
-I think you made
the right choice

00:01:22.182 --> 00:01:23.916
not even trying
to compete with her.

00:01:23.916 --> 00:01:25.217
-That's -- Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:01:25.217 --> 00:01:27.253
Ultimately, if you had been
like, "You know what?

00:01:27.253 --> 00:01:31.291
I'm going to be the dancer
they remember from tonight."

00:01:31.291 --> 00:01:32.491
-Yeah.

00:01:32.491 --> 00:01:34.627
-Hey, congratulations, as well,

00:01:34.627 --> 00:01:36.328
on your Critics's Choice
nomination today.

00:01:36.328 --> 00:01:37.764
-Thanks. Yep. Thanks.

00:01:37.764 --> 00:01:39.231
-Something you do have in common
with Janelle, so there you go.

00:01:39.231 --> 00:01:40.466
-Hey, yeah, okay.

00:01:40.466 --> 00:01:43.102
-But before I get to that,
your partner, Zoe Kazan,

00:01:43.102 --> 00:01:44.904
was here a couple weeks ago
talking about the fact

00:01:44.904 --> 00:01:46.805
that you have a very new baby.

00:01:46.805 --> 00:01:49.408
Congratulations on that.
-Thank you. Thanks.

00:01:49.408 --> 00:01:51.777
-You also have 4-year-old.

00:01:51.777 --> 00:01:55.181
How is the 4-year-old
adjusting to the new addition?

00:01:55.181 --> 00:01:58.450
-Well, the 4-year-old
has pinkeye

00:01:58.450 --> 00:02:01.554
and is sick right now --
not contagious, on antibiotics.

00:02:01.554 --> 00:02:04.057
-Great.
-Don't worry, Seth.

00:02:06.626 --> 00:02:09.729
With her medicine, actually,
you have to put it in the eye.

00:02:09.729 --> 00:02:10.930
The other day,
she was screaming,

00:02:10.930 --> 00:02:12.298
and I had to hold her down
to do it.

00:02:12.298 --> 00:02:14.500
And I said, "You know,
we're actually going to have to

00:02:14.500 --> 00:02:16.102
do this four times tomorrow."

00:02:16.102 --> 00:02:19.872
And she said,
"We're actually not."

00:02:19.872 --> 00:02:22.175
And I was like, "Four?"

00:02:22.175 --> 00:02:23.643
Like, nobody warned me.

00:02:23.643 --> 00:02:24.777
You hear about,
like, the terrible 2s,

00:02:24.777 --> 00:02:27.246
but 4 -- I mean,
4 has got some kick.

00:02:27.246 --> 00:02:29.449
-Terrible 2s is the
biggest lie they tell you,

00:02:29.449 --> 00:02:31.451
because you think to yourself --

00:02:31.451 --> 00:02:32.952
Because I've been
through it now twice.

00:02:32.952 --> 00:02:34.220
And you go, "Oh, great."

00:02:34.220 --> 00:02:36.155
And now 3,
and everything is easy.

00:02:36.155 --> 00:02:40.526
And then 3 is just the
terrible 2s but they're smarter.

00:02:40.526 --> 00:02:43.429
-That's so true.
-Yeah, they have more guile.

00:02:43.429 --> 00:02:44.897
It's awful.
-Yeah.

00:02:44.897 --> 00:02:47.967
-So, you -- This is a film --
This an autobiographical film

00:02:47.967 --> 00:02:49.035
by Steven Spielberg.

00:02:49.035 --> 00:02:51.303
You are playing
a version of his father.

00:02:51.303 --> 00:02:54.907
This is the first time you've
played a dad since being a dad.

00:02:54.907 --> 00:02:58.711
Does that affect
your approach to it at all?

00:02:58.711 --> 00:03:00.313
-Yeah.
It was actually really nice.

00:03:00.313 --> 00:03:02.715
I mean, the film I had
done before that, "The Batman,"

00:03:02.715 --> 00:03:05.918
I play The Riddler, and that's,
like, whole different bag.

00:03:05.918 --> 00:03:08.421
You're not taking your family
to work with you.

00:03:08.421 --> 00:03:11.156
If anything, you're trying
to create enough separation.

00:03:11.156 --> 00:03:13.759
So -- And this was kind of
the first time where

00:03:13.759 --> 00:03:17.996
it was not about my past or what
I'm digging up from my life

00:03:17.996 --> 00:03:19.165
or, like,, something.

00:03:19.165 --> 00:03:21.601
It was like,
"Oh, this is me now."

00:03:21.601 --> 00:03:23.603
I mean, it's not, but it is.
It's Burt.

00:03:23.603 --> 00:03:27.240
But, you know, what it means
to be in a marriage,

00:03:27.240 --> 00:03:29.976
to be a partner, to be a parent.

00:03:29.976 --> 00:03:31.744
And that felt really good to

00:03:31.744 --> 00:03:33.680
bring that part of myself
to work.

00:03:33.680 --> 00:03:35.414
-One of the great things
about this film

00:03:35.414 --> 00:03:37.750
is it talks about how,
you know, a father

00:03:37.750 --> 00:03:39.251
introduces his son to film,

00:03:39.251 --> 00:03:42.288
which becomes, like,
a life-long love affair.

00:03:42.288 --> 00:03:43.989
Do you --
Have you started introducing

00:03:43.989 --> 00:03:47.126
the 4-year-old to art yet?

00:03:47.126 --> 00:03:50.563
-Well, we do -- On the weekends,
we watch movies.

00:03:50.563 --> 00:03:52.264
We make popcorn
and we'll watch a movie.

00:03:52.264 --> 00:03:54.300
So, she gets, like, a little
more screen time on the weekend.

00:03:54.300 --> 00:03:55.668
-Yeah.
-So we'll do a movie.

00:03:55.668 --> 00:03:57.737
We haven't gone
to the theater yet.

00:03:57.737 --> 00:03:59.505
I don't know if that's because
she's, like, a pandemic kid.

00:03:59.505 --> 00:04:01.107
She was 1 1/2
when the pandemic hit.

00:04:01.107 --> 00:04:02.642
And, I mean,
I can't wait to do that.

00:04:02.642 --> 00:04:04.176
Her mind will be blown.

00:04:04.176 --> 00:04:06.712
I mean,
anticipation is not her thing.

00:04:06.712 --> 00:04:09.548
Like, so, you know,
it still needs to be

00:04:09.548 --> 00:04:12.351
a pretty benign,
you know, film, yeah.

00:04:12.351 --> 00:04:15.521
-Do you put a lot of --
Like, do you and Zoe --

00:04:15.521 --> 00:04:16.689
Do you stress about
picking the movie?

00:04:16.689 --> 00:04:17.890
Because that's where we are,

00:04:17.890 --> 00:04:19.191
because we don't have
a lot of screen time.

00:04:19.191 --> 00:04:20.259
So when we show them a movie,

00:04:20.259 --> 00:04:21.660
we want to make sure
it doesn't stink.

00:04:21.660 --> 00:04:24.930
-We stress, but there's so
many choices on these things,

00:04:24.930 --> 00:04:26.799
you know, and then
that's hard in itself,

00:04:26.799 --> 00:04:29.568
and then I get sucked into it
and you're scrolling forever.

00:04:29.568 --> 00:04:32.271
And then she's got her opinions,
which are very strong,

00:04:32.271 --> 00:04:35.341
as I've already, you know --
And, you know, usually,

00:04:35.341 --> 00:04:37.643
yeah, we just -- It's like,
"Okay, I want her to be happy."

00:04:37.643 --> 00:04:39.578
You know,
it's a tough line to --

00:04:39.578 --> 00:04:41.447
-I want them to be happy,
but, mostly,

00:04:41.447 --> 00:04:44.116
I just want them to be quiet
for two hours.

00:04:44.116 --> 00:04:45.618
-I'm glad you said it.

00:04:45.618 --> 00:04:47.620
-Yeah, just anything.
-I'm glad you said it.

00:04:47.620 --> 00:04:49.589
Yeah. Yeah.

00:04:49.589 --> 00:04:51.157
-I want it to be the,
like, kind of thing

00:04:51.157 --> 00:04:52.891
that will engage them but not be

00:04:52.891 --> 00:04:56.295
so smart they
have to ask me a question.

00:04:56.295 --> 00:04:57.930
Did you -- I mean,
it also must be crazy

00:04:57.930 --> 00:05:00.299
to be working with Spielberg,
because I'm assuming

00:05:00.299 --> 00:05:02.502
it's similar for both of us,
which is those are

00:05:02.502 --> 00:05:04.536
the movies I feel like
I grew up on.

00:05:04.536 --> 00:05:09.408
-One of my favorite
movie-theater memories is,

00:05:09.408 --> 00:05:10.943
I was living in a town
in Connecticut.

00:05:10.943 --> 00:05:13.012
"Jurassic Park"
was going to be so big.

00:05:13.012 --> 00:05:14.947
The lines were going to be
so big down the block.

00:05:14.947 --> 00:05:18.050
My mom took me out of school
that Friday early

00:05:18.050 --> 00:05:19.552
so I could go see the matinee.

00:05:19.552 --> 00:05:21.153
And for a kid
to get to miss school

00:05:21.153 --> 00:05:24.122
to go see a movie
was, like, the coolest thing.

00:05:24.122 --> 00:05:27.192
So that is one of my profound
theater-going memories

00:05:27.192 --> 00:05:28.694
is seeing "Jurassic Park."

00:05:28.694 --> 00:05:31.029
-You -- Those, of course,
were not real dinosaurs,

00:05:31.029 --> 00:05:34.633
but you did get to work with a
real monkey in this film.

00:05:34.633 --> 00:05:36.435
-What a transition, Seth. Wow.
-Thank you very much.

00:05:36.435 --> 00:05:39.539
-Yeah. Wow.
-This is not a CGI monkey.

00:05:39.539 --> 00:05:40.839
This is a real monkey.
-No.

00:05:40.839 --> 00:05:44.543
-And it turns out, a pretty
well-credentialed monkey.

00:05:44.543 --> 00:05:46.679
-Crystal's got
a better résumé than I do.

00:05:46.679 --> 00:05:52.151
-Crystal. This is Crystal.
Has own Wikipedia page.

00:05:52.151 --> 00:05:54.420
This, by the way --
IMDb page, as well.

00:05:54.420 --> 00:05:58.958
Can I read you something
that I found very --

00:05:58.958 --> 00:06:01.561
"Crystal the Monkey is
no small bananas.

00:06:01.561 --> 00:06:04.096
In just five of her movies,

00:06:04.096 --> 00:06:10.069
she holds a box-office gross
of over $2.5 billion."

00:06:10.069 --> 00:06:12.871
Like, I love that
she has a Hollywood publicist,

00:06:12.871 --> 00:06:14.507
who is, like,
adding up the movies

00:06:14.507 --> 00:06:16.642
and being like,
"It's over $2.5 billion."

00:06:16.642 --> 00:06:19.078
How was Crystal? Attitude? Diva?

00:06:19.078 --> 00:06:20.446
-Crystal was good.

00:06:20.446 --> 00:06:22.314
Crystal's trailer
was bigger than mine.

00:06:22.314 --> 00:06:24.717
But aside from that,
Crystal was good.

00:06:24.717 --> 00:06:26.452
You know, monkeys can be
very well-trained.

00:06:26.452 --> 00:06:27.686
You just can't train them
to go to the bathroom.

00:06:27.686 --> 00:06:28.988
-Uh-huh. Yep.

00:06:28.988 --> 00:06:30.422
-So they wear a diaper
when they're not on camera.

00:06:30.422 --> 00:06:31.858
-Right.

00:06:31.858 --> 00:06:33.125
-But when they are on camera,
they're wearing no diapers.

00:06:33.125 --> 00:06:34.293
So you just sort of --

00:06:34.293 --> 00:06:35.528
-You're just sort of
crossing your fingers?

00:06:35.528 --> 00:06:36.929
-You're on watch.
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:06:36.929 --> 00:06:38.364
-But you're also on
Steven's set,

00:06:38.364 --> 00:06:40.899
so you're going the act like you
are just going to do your job

00:06:40.899 --> 00:06:42.534
and buck up and -- Yeah.

00:06:42.534 --> 00:06:43.803
-Right. That's true.

00:06:43.803 --> 00:06:46.339
The things that Steven
has put his actors through

00:06:46.339 --> 00:06:48.106
over the years,
you can't be, like,

00:06:48.106 --> 00:06:49.575
squeamish about
a little monkey [bleep]

00:06:49.575 --> 00:06:52.144
-Yeah. That's right.
That's right. Yeah.

00:06:52.144 --> 00:06:54.547
-You did play The Riddler,
as you mentioned.

00:06:54.547 --> 00:06:56.749
And we talked about the fact
that it was a really cool thing

00:06:56.749 --> 00:06:58.984
to take on because you're
a comic-book fan.

00:06:58.984 --> 00:07:03.121
And then you wrote your
own Riddler comic, "Year One,"

00:07:03.121 --> 00:07:05.524
which is so wonderful.

00:07:05.524 --> 00:07:07.993
I know you worked on it
during the film, right?

00:07:07.993 --> 00:07:12.798
-Yeah. So, it started --
I do, like, a backstory

00:07:12.798 --> 00:07:14.566
where kind of to get to Page 1
of the script.

00:07:14.566 --> 00:07:15.901
And I like to try to
bring the life

00:07:15.901 --> 00:07:17.036
that's been
lived with me, right?

00:07:17.036 --> 00:07:18.270
And all that.

00:07:18.270 --> 00:07:20.372
And, so, I thought,
"Oh, it could be a comic."

00:07:20.372 --> 00:07:21.574
And, luckily, the director,
Matt Reeves,

00:07:21.574 --> 00:07:22.674
also said,
"That should be a comic."

00:07:22.674 --> 00:07:23.843
And I said, "Yeah."

00:07:23.843 --> 00:07:26.111
You know, that's secretly
what I was thinking.

00:07:26.111 --> 00:07:29.348
-I've been lucky enough to do
this once, as well.

00:07:29.348 --> 00:07:31.517
I think the
most exciting thing is when

00:07:31.517 --> 00:07:33.352
you actually get
the art back, right?

00:07:33.352 --> 00:07:35.521
I mean, you -- I'm not saying
it's easy to write a comic,

00:07:35.521 --> 00:07:37.322
but, obviously,
we both know the artist

00:07:37.322 --> 00:07:39.124
is working so much harder
than we are.

00:07:39.124 --> 00:07:42.862
And it is so -- Stevan did
such an incredible job.

00:07:42.862 --> 00:07:45.063
-Yeah, this guy, Stevan Subic --
He is a Serbian guy.

00:07:45.063 --> 00:07:46.532
He's not done a comic
here in the U.S.

00:07:46.532 --> 00:07:48.267
He is fantastic.
It's been so fun.

00:07:48.267 --> 00:07:50.335
When I get an e-mail
with a new page,

00:07:50.335 --> 00:07:52.438
I just light up, you know,
seeing what he has done.

00:07:52.438 --> 00:07:54.106
It's so fun.

00:07:54.106 --> 00:07:56.909
-And, yet, still can't
share it with your kids.

00:07:56.909 --> 00:07:58.944
Too adult for your kids.
-That's true.

00:07:58.944 --> 00:08:00.847
Not a bedtime story, that one.

00:08:00.847 --> 00:08:02.547
-It's pretty funny that you're
like, "Daddy writes comics."

00:08:02.547 --> 00:08:03.649
"Can we see them?"
"No."

00:08:03.649 --> 00:08:06.052
-Yeah. Yeah. Yeah.

00:08:06.052 --> 00:08:08.053
-"You can call up my friend
Crystal the Monkey.

00:08:08.053 --> 00:08:10.456
You can talk to her."

00:08:10.456 --> 00:08:12.024
Congratulations on the film.
-Thanks.

00:08:12.024 --> 00:08:13.459
-Congratulations on the comic

00:08:13.459 --> 00:08:14.726
and especially on your
growing family.

00:08:14.726 --> 00:08:15.928
Thanks for being here.
-Thanks. Appreciate it.

00:08:15.928 --> 00:08:18.631
-Always such a pleasure.
Paul Dano, everybody.

00:08:18.631 --> 00:08:21.500
"The Fabelmans" is in theaters
and on demand now.

00:08:21.500 --> 00:08:22.702
We'll be right back
with more "Late Night."

